{
  "skillId": "index-log-conversion",
  "atomType": "R",
  "theory": {
        "intro": "$a^{x}=y$ and $\\log_{a}y=x$ hold the same information in two layouts. To convert, find the base, the index and the number, then write them into the other form. Negative and fractional indices convert the same way.",
        "facts": [
          "In $a^{x}=y$ the base $a$ stays the base, the index $x$ becomes the value and the number $y$ becomes the argument of $\\log_{a}y=x$.",
          "Index to log: $5^{3}=125$ has base $5$, argument $125$ and value $3$, so it becomes $\\log_{5}125=3$.",
          "Log to index: in $\\log_{10}10000=4$ the base $10$ is raised to the value $4$ to give the argument, so $10^{4}=10000$.",
          "A negative index keeps its sign, so $10^{-2}=\\frac{1}{100}$ becomes $\\log_{10}\\frac{1}{100}=-2$.",
          "A fractional index works the same way, so $16^{\\frac{1}{2}}=4$ becomes $\\log_{16}4=\\frac{1}{2}$, and a root converts once written as $\\sqrt{5}=5^{\\frac{1}{2}}$.",
          "A zero index gives $7^{0}=1$, which becomes $\\log_{7}1=0$, because every base raised to the power $0$ is $1$."
        ],
        "steps": [
          "Identify the base, the index and the number in the statement.",
          "Write the equivalent statement using $a^{x}=y \\Leftrightarrow \\log_{a}y=x$."
        ]
      },
